<BaseLayout title="Inclusive EEG Electrode Hardware — Mishti Broor">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">EEG Hardware · Liu-CoMotion Lab · UIC</div><h1>Designing EEG hardware for textured hair.</h1><p>Engineering and validating novel electrode attachments for unbraided and cornrowed hair — from CAD and resin 3D printing through a 30-participant multimodal human study.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section project-media"><div class="container"><img class="hero-image" src={`${base}images/eeg-poster-solo.jpeg`} alt="Mishti Broor presenting research on novel EEG electrodes for Afro-textured hair" /></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container prose">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>Reliable EEG acquisition depends on the interface between the electrode and scalp. Dense, textured, braided, and cornrowed hair can make conventional cap electrodes harder to place consistently, creating a hardware and human-factors challenge alongside the electrical one.</p>

    <h2>My role</h2>
    <p>I worked across the project cycle — hardware design, fabrication, experimental setup, participant testing, and signal analysis.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Engineered and fabricated novel EEG electrode attachments using CAD and resin 3D printing, with compatibility for 10–20 and 10–05 placement systems.</li>
      <li>Helped conduct a 30-subject, two-phase study using high-density BrainVision EEG, IMUs, and force-sensor insoles across rest, overground walking, and treadmill trials.</li>
      <li>Led experimental preparation including digital electrode mapping, gel application, and impedance optimization below 15 kΩ.</li>
      <li>Built and debugged a MATLAB–Python analysis pipeline using individual alpha frequency estimation and FOOOF-corrected SNR modeling to compare the novel system against a conventional EEG cap.</li>
      <li>Contributed to manuscript preparation, statistical reporting, figures, and IRB-compliant data management.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section project-media"><div class="container two-up"><img src={`${base}images/eeg-poster-discussion.jpeg`} alt="Mishti discussing the EEG electrode project during a research presentation" /><img src={`${base}images/eeg-poster-team.jpeg`} alt="Mishti and a collaborator presenting the EEG electrode project" /></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="container prose">
    <h2>Why this project shaped my direction</h2>
    <p>The project made bioelectronics feel concrete to me. Signal quality was not determined by software alone: electrode geometry, scalp contact, participant variability, comfort, movement, placement repeatability, and downstream analysis all interacted.</p>
    <p>That systems-level challenge is what pushed me toward wearable neural sensing and bioelectronic hardware designed to work beyond tightly controlled laboratory environments.</p>

    <h2>Tools & methods</h2>
    <p><strong>Hardware:</strong> CAD, resin 3D printing, high-density BrainVision EEG, IMUs, force-sensor insoles.</p>
    <p><strong>Analysis:</strong> MATLAB, Python, digital filtering, individual alpha frequency estimation, FOOOF/specparam-style spectral modeling, SNR analysis.</p>
    <p><strong>Research:</strong> Human-subject protocol execution, impedance optimization, synchronized multimodal acquisition, IRB-compliant data management.</p>
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
